  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Sex Sounds shows an early but meaningful scholarly uptake, with three OpenAlex citations already distributed across 2023–2025. The citing works come from music scholarship and adjacent critical fields, and the book’s subject matter aligns with a clear social theme: reduced inequalities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Danielle Shlomit Sofer’s Sex Sounds is beginning to circulate within scholarly conversations on electronic music, gender, and queer and trans-oriented critique. The record shows three citations to date, which is a modest but real sign of engagement for a 2022 scholarly book. Those citations appear in consecutive years from 2023 through 2025, suggesting that attention has been steady rather than isolated.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape is disciplinary and focused. The book is being cited in a mix of journal articles and a book chapter, including venues such as Contemporary Music Review, Music Theory Spectrum, and Ästhetiken X.0. The topics of the citing works indicate that Sofer’s argument is resonating in musicology, queer theory, and debates on musical interpretation and institutions, rather than remaining confined to a single niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book is also available open access through hybrid access routes, including an open book instance in the record and a listing in the Directory of Open Access Books. That openness may support discovery and reuse, and it fits the broader profile of a work concerned with inclusion and critical reevaluation of music history.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond citations, the record shows a small but identifiable signal of reader recognition: a LibraryThing entry notes it as a finalist for the ACLS Open Access Book Prize &amp; Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award in the Literary/Media Studies category in 2026. Teaching adoption evidence is limited to library holdings rather than explicit course listings, so the safest reading is that the book has entered circulation in research and library contexts, while wider pedagogical uptake is not yet documented in the available record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, Sex Sounds appears to be an emergent scholarly reference point for work on electronic music, sexuality, and critical music studies. The evidence is still early, but it shows the book gaining traction in a focused set of academic conversations with relevance to questions of identity, representation, and inequality.</p>",
